+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more integers each followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours, `m`
+for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once, in that order, with no spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive. For example, `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, and `1d2h3m4s` are valid, while `30m1h`, `1h1h`, `1h 30m`,
+`1.5h`, and `-5` are not. A `timedelta` default is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h30m"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=90"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1.5h"])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=["--help"])
+```
+
